    <node id="radiology">
      <data key="d0">radiology</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiology is a distinct medical specialty that has evolved over time, with its origins dating back to the late 19th century. It involves the use of various medical imaging modalities, including X-rays, computed tomography (CT) scans, magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), and positron emission tomography (PET), to diagnose and treat diseases. Today, radiology encompasses a broad range of techniques, including plain radiography, ultrasound, MRI, CT, and nuclear medicine. The field has also become increasingly sophisticated, with advancements in computer-aided detection and image-guided interventions.

Radiology professionals use their knowledge and skills to interpret diagnostic imaging exams, which helps doctors to identify and manage various conditions, ranging from fractures and tumors to cardiovascular and neurological disorders. In addition to diagnosis, radiology plays a critical role in minimally invasive treatments, such as biopsies, vascular interventions, and tumor ablation therapies. Moreover, radiologists work hand-in-hand with other healthcare specialists to develop personalized treatment plans tailored to the unique needs of each patient.

Throughout its history, radiology has undergone many transformations and innovations, driven by technological advancements and a deeper understanding of human biology and disease processes. Today, image-guided interventions represent an essential aspect of radiology, allowing clinicians to target and perform procedures without the need for open surgery. Ongoing research is focused on developing advanced imaging techniques and computational methods to improve diagnostic accuracy, streamline patient care, and ultimately save lives. With its critical role in medicine and continuous evolution, radiology will undoubtedly continue to shape the field of healthcare for years to come.&lt;SEP&gt;The study or application of medical imaging to diagnose and treat diseases and conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-cd00db0ae9ecbe44b58bb940c8d22e91-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ddb189d08becfbc31626442a6e6080f1-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70206bf5a955ae83322d2cf6035a3ef9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a6b1da4c7da3290d7d05a67bc6887c2b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-95b580a63c7343d8b7f976e925b74120-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-427e6ff68cdb25df461dc4bf22a75452-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-6f41dbcd0ad3671a2ac5da13460b3f44-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b11d21ed4c1de5a364f9078d6a43e651-chunk-001</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1790410891</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="x_ray">
      <data key="d0">x_ray</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">A diagnostic imaging technique using ionizing radiation to produce images of the internal structures of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging modality using X-ray technology to visualize internal structures.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical imaging technique that uses ionizing radiation to produce images of the inside of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiographic imaging technique that uses X-rays to produce images of internal structures of the body, primarily for diagnostic purposes.</data>
